# Resolved by `op run` at the moment tofu starts. Committed on purpose: every
# op:// below is a REFERENCE, not a value, and resolving one requires an
# unlocked 1Password account this file knows nothing about.
#
#   op run --env-file=op.env -- tofu plan
#
# Vault, item AND item-type are all a contract here. Renaming any of them breaks
# the apply, so grep this file first. See ../docs/CREDENTIALS.md.
#
# The item TYPE decides the field names, which is the non-obvious half:
#
#   Cloudflare API token      API Credential   username + credential
#   R2 tfstate token          API Credential   username + credential
#   Infomaniak Public Cloud   Login            username + password + project
#
# Login for Infomaniak because that credential is also what signs into the
# Horizon dashboard, and because a Login has `password` natively — an API
# Credential item calls that field `credential`, and OS_PASSWORD below would
# then resolve to nothing.
#
# `project` is a custom field added to that item. ⚠ Add it OUTSIDE any section:
# a field inside a section needs the section in the path
# (op://Engineering/Item/Section/project) and the reference below would miss it.
#
# Don't hand-write these. In the 1Password app, right-click any field and
# "Copy Secret Reference" — that is the authoritative form.
#
# Everything below lives in ONE vault on purpose. When a service account is
# eventually created for CI, it gets read access to Engineering and nothing
# else — least privilege by construction rather than by an audit later.

# ─── Cloudflare ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Zone > DNS > Edit on agentsee.work. NOT the Pages-only CI token, which lives
# in GitHub Actions secrets and cannot write DNS.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=op://Engineering/Cloudflare API token/credential

# ─── R2, for the state backend only ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The backend speaks S3, so it reads the standard AWS variables. This is the
# tfstate token — the BACKUP bucket has a different token that lives on the
# box, never here. One credential that could both delete the backups and
# rewrite the infrastructure is a bad blast radius.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=op://Engineering/R2 tfstate token/username
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=op://Engineering/R2 tfstate token/credential

# ─── Infomaniak Public Cloud (OpenStack) ─────────────────────────────────────
# The provider reads these directly, so there is no clouds.yaml on disk holding
# a password. Not secret, so inline:
OS_AUTH_URL=https://api.pub1.infomaniak.cloud/identity/v3
OS_REGION_NAME=dc3-a
OS_INTERFACE=public
OS_IDENTITY_API_VERSION=3

# Taken from a real downloaded clouds.yaml, not from the docs. Both domains are
# LOWERCASE `default` — Keystone domain names are case-sensitive, and `Default`
# fails with a bare 401 that names nothing it could have been. The auth URL
# carries the /v3 suffix; Infomaniak's Terraform page omits it and that works
# too, but this matches the file you download, so the two agree when compared.
#
# ⚠ username is PCU-…, project is PCP-… — they differ by one letter. Putting
# the PCU value in both fields also gives a bare 401.
#
# ⚠ The PASSWORD is the PCU user's own, set when the project was created — NOT
# your Infomaniak account password. If it was never set, the manager sends a
# link by email to set it.
OS_USERNAME=op://Engineering/Infomaniak Public Cloud/username
OS_PASSWORD=op://Engineering/Infomaniak Public Cloud/password
OS_PROJECT_NAME=op://Engineering/Infomaniak Public Cloud/project
OS_USER_DOMAIN_NAME=default
OS_PROJECT_DOMAIN_NAME=default
